In the midst of last week's selloff UBS’s wealth management arm cut U.S. stocks to Neutral from Attractive in its 2027 outlook, saying the bar for companies to deliver is now high.  Its 8,200 end-2027 S&P 500 target ties Wells Fargo for the lowest on the Street, about 500 points under the roughly 8,700 median (MarketWatch).

They present, though, four scenarios which point to a lower number:

▪️ Base case: 8,200, 50% odds;
▪️ Upside: 9,500, 20%;
▪️ AI spending slows sharply: 5,500, 20% (it was 10% in late August, per Bloomberg Línea);
▪️ Stubborn inflation: 7,000, 10%.

Weight those by UBS’s odds and you get about 7,800, only about 6.5% above Wednesday’s 7,324 close. The two downside cases now carry 30% odds against 20% for the upside. And the most bearish case sits 2,700 points below the base, while the upside is only 1,300 above it.

They say it's not about earnings. UBS still sees S&P 500 EPS up about 29% this year and 15% in 2027, to $425. The worry is that the top 10 stocks are about 40% of the index, so a few estimate cuts move everything.

Also note this is the wealth arm. UBS Investment Bank’s Keith Parker is still at 8,900. The February U.S. downgrade you may remember was the investment bank’s, not this one.

UBS isn’t saying sell. It says keep core U.S. exposure but use strength to trim megacap concentration, look at equal-weight, and favor the Eurozone, industrials and health care.

